    "analysis": "Gold has historically proven to be a reliable inflation hedge due to its store-of-value properties and inverse relationship with inflation. With a maximum allocation of 7.5% for Gold, I recommend allocating 5% to this asset to serve as a hedge against inflation, maintaining a diversified portfolio. As an inflation hedge, Gold has demonstrated a correlation coefficient of around -0.6 with inflation, indicating a significant inverse relationship. This inverse correlation suggests that as inflation increases, the value of Gold tends to rise, potentially helping stabilize the portfolio's purchasing power.\n\nInflation correlation rationale: Gold's price tends to move inversely with inflation rates, as higher inflation erodes the value of fiat currencies, causing investors to seek safe-haven assets like Gold. This inverse relationship provides a stable store-of-value and hedging mechanism against inflation.\n\nConstraint flags: rebalance_drift_threshold (if actual exposure exceeds 7.5%), max_total_allocation (potential maximum allocation limit of 15%)\n\nRecommendation: buy\n\nProposed allocation: [0.05]\n\nConfidence: high\n\nOut of scope: false",
